    <node><name>2</name><description>Four sides</description><answertest>AlgEquiv</answertest><sans>ans1</sans><tans>Afour</tans><testoptions></testoptions><quiet>1</quiet><truescoremode>=</truescoremode><truescore>0.0000000</truescore><truepenalty></truepenalty><truenextnode>-1</truenextnode><trueanswernote>prt1-3-T</trueanswernote><truefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>That treats the pen as fenced on all four sides. Here the wall is one side, so all \({@L0@}\) m goes on three sides.</p>]]></text></truefeedback><falsescoremode>=</falsescoremode><falsescore>0.0000000</falsescore><falsepenalty></falsepenalty><falsenextnode>-1</falsenextnode><falseanswernote>prt1-3-F</falseanswernote><falsefeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[[[if test="is(chk#0)"]]<p>Test your expression with \(x = {@L0/2@}\): the two sides that meet the wall then use all \({@L0@}\) m of fence, so nothing is left for the third side and the area must be \(0\). Your expression gives \({@chk@}\).</p>[[/if]]<p>Draw the pen. Write the length of the side along the wall in terms of \(x\), then multiply.</p>]]></text></falsefeedback></node>
